# Introduction # {#intro}

*This section is non-normative.*

The [Graphics Processing Unit](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graphics_processing_unit), or GPU, is essential for enabling rich rendering and computation applications in personal computing.
WebGPU is a set of APIs that expose GPU hardware capabilities to the web. The API is designed from the ground up to efficiently map to the (post-2014) native GPU APIs. WebGPU is not related to [WebGL](https://www.khronos.org/webgl/) and does not correspond to OpenGL ES.

WebGPU treats the physical GPU hardware as a {{GPUAdapter}}. It connects to the adapter through a {{GPUDevice}}, which can be used to manage resources, while the device's {{GPUQueue}} can be used to execute commands. The {{GPUDevice}} may have its own memory with high-speed access to the processing units. {{GPUBuffer}} and {{GPUTexture}} are the two kinds of <dfn dfn>physical resources</dfn> in GPU memory. {{GPUCommandBuffer}} and {{GPURenderBundle}} are containers used to store user-recorded commands. {{GPUShaderModule}} is used to store [=shader=] code. Other resources are configured in the way the GPU uses [=physical resources=], such as {{GPUSampler}} or {{GPUBindGroup}}.

The GPU executes the commands encoded in a {{GPUCommandBuffer}} by feeding data into a [=pipeline=], which is a mixture of fixed-function and programmable stages. In the programmable stages, the GPU can run <dfn dfn>shader</dfn> languages, which are special programs designed to run on GPU hardware. Most of the state of a [=pipeline=] is defined by a {{GPURenderPipeline}} or {{GPUComputePipeline}} object. The state that is not in these [=pipeline=] objects is set during encoding through the use of commands, such as {{GPUCommandEncoder/beginRenderPass()}} or {{GPURenderPassEncoder/setBlendConstant()}}.


# Malicious use considerations # {#malicious-use}

*This section is non-normative.* This section describes the risks associated with exposing this API on the Web.

## Security ## {#security}

The security requirements for WebGPU are the same as for the rest of the Web environment, and they must be strictly enforced. The general approach to fulfilling the security requirements is to strictly validate all commands before they reach the GPU, so that a page can only access its own data.

### CPU-based undefined behavior ### {#security-cpu-ub}

A WebGPU implementation translates the workloads submitted by the user into API commands specific to the target platform. The rules for valid usage of these API commands are defined by the native APIs. (See, for example, [vkCreateDescriptorSetLayout](https://www.khronos.org/registry/vulkan/specs/1.2-extensions/man/html/vkCreateDescriptorSetLayout.html).) When the rules for valid usage are not followed, the result of an API command is generally not guaranteed. This is called "undefined behavior", and it can be exploited by an attacker to access memory they don't have permission to, or to force the driver to execute malicious code.

In order to disallow insecure usage, any workload submitted by the user needs to be defined within the range of behavior allowed by WebGPU. A WebGPU implementation must validate all the workloads submitted by the user, so that only valid workloads are executed by the driver. This document defines all the error scenarios and the ways they are handled. For example, specifying the same buffer with intersecting ranges in both "source" and "destination" of {{GPUCommandEncoder/copyBufferToBuffer()}} causes the {{GPUCommandEncoder}} to generate an error, and no further operations are performed.

See [[#errors-and-debugging]] for more information about error handling.

## GPU-based undefined behavior ## {#security-gpu-ub}

WebGPU [=shader=] programs are executed by the compute units inside the GPU hardware. In native APIs, some shader instructions may result in undefined behavior on the GPU. To address this, the set of shader instructions and their defined behavior are strictly specified by WebGPU. When shader code is provided to {{GPUDevice/createShaderModule()}}, the WebGPU implementation must validate it before performing any translation (to platform-specific shaders).

## Uninitialized data ## {#security-uninitialized}

Generally, allocating new memory may expose the residual data of other applications running on the system. To address this, WebGPU conceptually initializes all resources to zero, although in practice this step may be skipped if the developer initializes the contents manually. The contents that need to be initialized include variables inside shaders and shared workgroup memory.

The precise mechanism for clearing workgroup memory may vary from platform to platform. If a platform's native API does not provide a mechanism to clear workgroup memory, the WebGPU implementation on this platform transforms the processing of a compute shader so that: it first clears all the invocations, synchronously confirms that all invocations are cleared, and only then proceeds to execute the developer's code.

## Out-of-bounds access in shaders ## {#security-shader}

[=shader=] programs can access [=physical resources=] directly (for example, as a {{GPUBufferBindingType/"uniform"}} {{GPUBufferBinding}}), or through <dfn dfn>texture units</dfn>, which are fixed-function hardware blocks that handle the transformation of texture coordinates. Validation on the API side can only guarantee that all the inputs provided to a shader have the correct usage and type.
Without invoking a [=texture unit=], the host API side cannot guarantee that the data is accessed within bounds.

Issue: define how a host API differs from a shader API

To prevent a shader from accessing GPU memory that an application doesn't have permission to, a WebGPU implementation may enable a special mode (called "robust buffer access") in the driver, which guarantees that shader accesses stay within the buffer bounds.

Another way to prevent out-of-bounds accesses in shaders is for a WebGPU implementation to transform the shader code by inserting manual bounds checks. When this approach is taken, the bounds checks only apply to array indexing. Ordinary field accesses of shader structures don't need to be checked, because the validation of {{GPUBufferBindingLayout/minBindingSize}} is done on the host side.

If a shader attempts to load data outside of the [=physical resource=] bounds, the WebGPU implementation may allow:
  1. returning a value at another location within the resource bounds
  2. returning a value vector of "(0, 0, 0, X)" with any arbitrary "X"
  3. partially discarding the draw or dispatch call

If a shader attempts to write data outside of the [=physical resource=] bounds, the WebGPU implementation may allow:
  1. writing the value to another location within the resource bounds
  2. discarding the write operation
  3. partially discarding the draw or dispatch call

## Invalid data ## {#security-invalid-data}

When uploading [floating-point](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IEEE_754) data from the CPU to the GPU, or generating floating-point numbers on the GPU, we may end up with a binary representation that does not correspond to a valid number, such as infinity or NaN (not a number). The GPU behavior in this case is subject to the precision of the GPU hardware implementation, which is based on the IEEE-754 standard. WebGPU guarantees that introducing invalid floating-point numbers only affects the results of the arithmetic computations and has no other side effects.

### Driver bugs ### {#security-driver-bugs}

GPU drivers are susceptible to bugs like any other software. If a bug occurs, an attacker could exploit the incorrect behavior of the driver to access data they don't have permission to. In order to reduce this risk, the WebGPU working group will coordinate with GPU vendors to integrate the WebGPU Conformance Test Suite (CTS) as part of their driver testing process, similar to the way WebGL drivers are tested. WebGPU implementations will provide workarounds for some of the discovered bugs, and disable WebGPU on drivers where the known bugs cannot be worked around.

### Timing attacks ### {#security-timing}

WebGPU is used in a multi-threaded manner through Web Workers. Therefore, WebGPU is designed to protect users from modern high-precision timing attacks. Some objects, such as {{GPUBuffer}} or {{GPUQueue}}, have shared state that can be accessed concurrently. Race conditions can therefore occur, similar to multiple Web Workers accessing the same "SharedArrayBuffer" concurrently, in which case the thread scheduling is observable.

WebGPU addresses this issue by only granting the ability to deserialize (or share) objects to [=agents=] in an [=agent cluster=] when the rules of [cross-origin isolation](https://web.dev/coop-coep/) are enabled. This restriction is consistent with the [mitigations](https://hacks.mozilla.org/2020/07/safely-reviving-shared-memory/) against malicious use of "SharedArrayBuffer". Similarly, a user agent may serialize the handles shared by an [=agent=] to completely prevent any concurrency.

In summary, the race attacks that WebGPU shared state may be subject to are a small subset of the `SharedArrayBuffer` attacks.

WebGPU also specifies the {{GPUFeatureName/"timestamp-query"}} feature, which provides high-precision timing of GPU operations. This feature is optional, and a WebGPU implementation may only expose it in trusted scenarios. Alternatively, the timestamp query results can be processed by a compute shader and aligned to a lower precision.

### Row hammer attacks ### {#security-rowhammer}

[Row hammer](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Row_hammer) is a class of attacks that exploit the leakage of state in DRAM cells. It can be used [on GPUs](https://www.vusec.net/projects/glitch/). WebGPU does not have any specific mitigations in place, and relies on platform-level solutions, such as reduced memory refresh intervals.

## Denial of service ## {#security-dos}

WebGPU applications have access to GPU memory and compute units. A WebGPU implementation may limit
the GPU memory available to an application in order to keep other applications responsive. For GPU processing time, a WebGPU implementation may set up "watchdog" timers to ensure that an application doesn't cause the GPU to become unresponsive for more than a few seconds. These measures are similar to those used in WebGL.

## Workload identification ## {#security-workload-identification}

WebGPU provides access to constrained global resources that are shared between different programs (and web pages) running on the same machine. Based on the usage patterns of these shared resources, an application can attempt to indirectly probe how these global resources are constrained, in order to infer the workloads performed by other open web pages. These problems are generally similar to those faced by Javascript, such as system memory and CPU execution throughput. WebGPU does not provide any additional
mitigations for this.

### Memory resources ### {#security-memory-resources}

WebGPU exposes the ability to attempt failing allocations from the machine's global memory heaps, such as VRAM. By attempting to allocate memory resources and detecting whether the allocation fails, the amount of remaining available memory (for a given heap type) on the system can be probed.

A GPU has one or more (usually only two) memory heaps that are shared by all running applications. When a heap is exhausted, WebGPU will fail to create resources. The allocation of these memory heaps is observable, which may allow a malicious application to guess which heaps are used by other applications, and how many resources those applications have allocated from them.

### Computation resources ### {#security-computation-resources}

If one site uses WebGPU concurrently with another site, this site will find that the time it takes to process some work increases. For example, if a site continuously submits compute workloads and tracks the completion of work on its queue, it may observe that other workloads also start to use the GPU.

A GPU has many parts that can be tested independently, such as the arithmetic units, texture sampling units, atomic units, and so on. A malicious application may sense that some of these units are under stress, and try to guess the workload of another application by analyzing the stress patterns. This is similar to the way actual Javascript CPU execution works.

## Privacy ## {#security-privacy}

WebGPU can expose many details about the underlying GPU architecture and the geometry of the device. This includes the available physical adapters, many of the limits on the resources usable by the GPU and CPU (such as the maximum texture size), and any optional hardware-specific capabilities that are available.

User agents are not obligated to expose the true hardware limits; they have full control over how much of the machine's details are exposed. One strategy to reduce fingerprinting is binning, placing all target platforms into a few bins. In general, the privacy impact of exposing hardware limits matches that of WebGL.

The [=limit/default=] limits are also deliberately set to be generous enough to allow most applications to work without needing to request higher limits. All usage of the API is validated against the requested limits, so the actual hardware capabilities are not accidentally exposed to the user.

### Machine-specific artifacts ### {#security-machine-artifacts}

There are some machine-specific rasterization/precision artifacts and performance differences that can also be observed in WebGL. These machine-specific artifacts apply to rasterization coverage and patterns, the interpolation precision of varyings between shader stages, compute unit scheduling, and more aspects of execution.

In general, the rasterization and precision fingerprints are identical across most or all of the devices of a given vendor. Performance differences are trickier, but the differences are also relatively small (as with JS execution performance).

### Machine-specific performance ### {#security-machine-performance}

Another factor for distinguishing users is measuring the performance of specific operations on the GPU. Even with low timing precision, repeatedly executing an operation can reveal whether a user's machine is fast at executing a specific workload. This is a fairly common vector (present in WebGL and Javascript), but it is also low-signal and relatively hard to actually standardize on.

WebGPU compute pipelines expose access to the GPU, unimpeded by fixed-function hardware. This poses an additional risk for unique device fingerprinting. A user agent may take steps to decouple the logical GPU invocations from the actual compute units in order to reduce this risk.

### User agent state ### {#security-user-agent-state}

This specification does not define any additional user agent state.
However, user agents are required to provide a compilation cache for expensive compilations, such as {{GPUShaderModule}}, {{GPURenderPipeline}}, and {{GPUComputePipeline}}. These caches are important for improving the loading time of WebGPU applications after the first visit's load.

For the specification, these caches are indistinguishable from extremely fast compilations, but for an application, it is easy to measure how long {{GPUDevice/createComputePipelineAsync()}} takes to execute. This may leak information across origins (for example, "did the user visit the site using this particular shader"), so user agents should follow the best practices in [storage partitioning](https://github.com/privacycg/storage-partitioning).

The system's GPU driver may also have its own compiled shader and pipeline caches. User agents
may wish to disable these where possible, or take approaches that make the GPU driver consider them different, by adding per-partition data to the shaders.

### Adapter identifiers ### {#privacy-adapter-identifiers}

Issue: Describe the considerations for exposing adapter information

Note: It is expected that WebGPU will expose some level of information to identify the type of GPU adapter that is being used.
This is a potential source of fingerprinting information, but past experience with WebGL has shown that, to some extent, it is necessary to enable developers to create robust applications and respond effectively to user issues.

# Fundamentals # {#fundamentals}

## Conventions ## {#api-conventions}

### Dot syntax ### {#dot-syntax}

In this specification, the `.` ("dot syntax") notation commonly used in programming languages is used. The phrase "`Foo.Bar`" means "the `Bar` member of `Foo`".

The `?.` ("optional chaining operator") syntax adopted in JavaScript is also used. The phrase "Foo?.Bar" means, "if `Foo` is `null` or `undefined`, the result is `undefined`; otherwise, the result is `Foo.Bar`".

For example, if `buffer` is a {{GPUBuffer}}, `buffer?.[[device]].[[adapter]]` means, "if `buffer` is `null` or `undefined`, then buffer is `undefined`; otherwise, return the `[[adapter]]` internal to the `[[device]]` internal to buffer."

### Internal objects ### {#webgpu-internal-objects}

An <dfn dfn>internal object</dfn> is a conceptual, non-exposed WebGPU object. An [=internal object=] tracks the state of an API object and encompasses all of the potential API implementation. If the state of a particular [=internal object=] can be changed concurrently from multiple [=agents=], those changes are always the smallest executable unit with respect to all [=agents=].

Note: An "[=agent=]" refers to a Javascript "thread" (i.e. the main thread, or a Web Worker).

### WebGPU interfaces ### {#webgpu-interfaces}

A <dfn dfn>WebGPU interface</dfn> is an exposed interface that encapsulates an [=internal object=]. The state of an [=internal object=] is changed through the interfaces provided by WebGPU.

Every interface that includes {{GPUObjectBase}} is a [=WebGPU interface=].

<script type=idl>
interface mixin GPUObjectBase {
    attribute USVString? label;
};
</script>

{{GPUObjectBase}} has the following attributes:

<dl dfn-type=attribute dfn-for=GPUObjectBase>
    : <dfn>label</dfn>
    ::
        A label that is used in developer tools (such as error/warning messages, browser developer tools, and platform debugging tools) to identify the underlying [=internal object=] to the developer. The label has no specific format, so it cannot be reliably parsed by a machine.

        In any case, developers are free to choose whether to use the label field.
</dl>

{{GPUObjectBase}} has the following internal slots:

<dl dfn-type=attribute dfn-for=GPUObjectBase>
    : <dfn>\[[device]]</dfn>, of type [=device=], readonly
    ::
        An internal slot holding a [=device=], the [=device=] that also owns the [=internal object=].
</dl>

### Object descriptors ### {#object-descriptors}

An <dfn dfn>object descriptor</dfn> holds the information needed to create an object, which is usually done via one of the `create*` methods of {{GPUDevice}}.

<script type=idl>
dictionary GPUObjectDescriptorBase {
    USVString label;
};
</script>

{{GPUObjectDescriptorBase}} has the following members:

<dl dfn-type=dict-member dfn-for=GPUObjectDescriptorBase>
    : <dfn>label</dfn>
    ::
        The initial value of {{GPUObjectBase/label|GPUObjectBase.label}}.
</dl>

## Invalid internal objects &amp; contagious invalidity ## {#invalidity}

Object creation operations in WebGPU are internally asynchronous, so they do not fail with exceptions. Instead, the returned object may reference a <dfn dfn>valid</dfn> or <dfn dfn>invalid</dfn> [=internal object=]. An [=invalid=] object may never later become [=valid=]. Some objects may become [=invalid=] during their lifetime, while most may be [=invalid=] only at creation time.

If an object cannot be created, the object is [=invalid=] from the moment of creation. This can happen, for example, if the [=object descriptor=] does not describe a valid object, or if there is not enough memory to allocate the resource.

*Most* types of [=internal object=] do not become [=invalid=] after creation, but may still become unusable, for example if the owning device is [=lose the device|lost=] or {{GPUDevice/destroy()|destroyed}}, or if the object has a special internal state, such as a buffer whose state is corrupted.

Some types of [=internal object=] *may* become [=invalid=] after creation; specifically, devices, adapters, and command/pass/bundle encoders.

<div>
    A given {{GPUObjectBase}} |object| is <dfn abstract-op>valid to use with</dfn> a |targetObject| when, and only when, the following requirements are met:

    <div class=validusage>
        - |object| must be [=valid=].
        - |object|.{{GPUObjectBase/[[device]]}} must be [=valid=].
        - |object|.{{GPUObjectBase/[[device]]}} must equal |targetObject|.{{GPUObjectBase/[[device]]}}.
    </div>
</div>

## Coordinate Systems ## {#coordinate-systems}

  - Y-axis is up in normalized device coordinate (NDC): point(-1.0, -1.0) in NDC is located at the bottom-left corner of NDC. In addition, x and y in NDC should be between -1.0 and 1.0 inclusive, while z in NDC should be between 0.0 and 1.0 inclusive. Vertices out of this range in NDC will not introduce any errors, but they will be clipped.
  - Y-axis is down in [=framebuffer=] coordinate, viewport coordinate and fragment/pixel coordinate: origin(0, 0) is located at the top-left corner in these coordinate systems.
  - Window/present coordinate matches [=framebuffer=] coordinate.
  - The origin(0, 0) in UV of texture coordinate represents the first texel (the lowest byte) in texture memory.

Note: WebGPU's coordinate systems match DirectX's coordinate systems in a graphics pipeline.

## Programming Model ## {#programming-model}

### Timelines ### {#programming-model-timelines}

*This section is non-normative.*

A computer system with a user agent at the front-end and a GPU at the back-end has components that work in parallel on different timelines:

: <dfn dfn>content timeline</dfn>
:: Associated with the execution of the Web script. It includes calling all the methods described by this specification.

    <div class=content-timeline>
        Steps executed on the content timeline are shown like this.
    </div>

: <dfn dfn>device timeline</dfn>
:: Associated with the GPU device operations issued by the user agent. It includes creation of adapters, devices, and GPU resources and state objects, which are typically synchronous operations from the point of view of the user agent part that controls the GPU, but can live in a separate operating system process.

    <div class=device-timeline>
        Steps executed on the device timeline are shown like this.
    </div>

: <dfn dfn>queue timeline</dfn>
:: Associated with the execution of operations on the compute units of the GPU. It includes the actual draw, copy, and compute jobs that run on the GPU.

    <div class=queue-timeline>
        Steps executed on the queue timeline are shown like this.
    </div>

In this specification, asynchronous operations are used when the resulting value depends on work that happens on any timeline other than the [=content timeline=]. They are represented by callbacks and promises in JavaScript.

<div class="example">
{{GPUComputePassEncoder/dispatch(x, y, z)|GPUComputePassEncoder.dispatch()}}:

  1. The user encodes a `dispatch` command by calling a {{GPUComputePassEncoder}} method, which happens on the [=content timeline=].
  2. The user calls {{GPUQueue/submit(commandBuffers)|GPUQueue.submit()}}, handing the {{GPUCommandBuffer}} over to the user agent, which performs the low-level submission on the [=device timeline=] by calling the operating system driver.
  3. On the [=queue timeline=], the submission is dispatched by the GPU scheduler onto the actual compute units for execution.

</div>
<div class="example">
{{GPUDevice/createBuffer(descriptor)|GPUDevice.createBuffer()}}:

  1. On the [=content timeline=], the user fills in a {{GPUBufferDescriptor}} and uses it to create a {{GPUBuffer}}.
  2. The user agent creates a low-level buffer on the [=device timeline=].

</div>
<div class="example">
{{GPUBuffer/mapAsync()|GPUBuffer.mapAsync()}}:

  1. The user requests to map the {{GPUBuffer}} on the [=content timeline=], and gets a promise as the return value.
  2. The user agent checks whether the buffer is currently used by the GPU, and reminds itself to check again once that usage is finished.
  3. After the GPU operations on the [=queue timeline=] that use the buffer are complete, the user agent maps it into memory and [=resolve|resolves=] the promise.

</div>

### Memory Model ### {#programming-model-memory}

*This section is non-normative.*

Once a {{GPUDevice}} has been obtained during an application initialization routine, we can describe the <dfn dfn>WebGPU platform</dfn> as consisting of the following layers:
  1. The user agent, implementing the specification.
  2. The operating system, with low-level native API drivers for this device.
  3. The actual CPU and GPU hardware.

Each layer of the [=WebGPU platform=] may have different memory types that the user agent needs to consider when implementing the specification:
  - The GPU driver usually cannot access memory owned by the script, such as an {{ArrayBuffer}} created by the script.
  - The user agent may have different processes responsible for running the content, and for communicating with the GPU driver. In this case, the user agent uses inter-process shared memory to transfer data.
  - A discrete GPU has its own high-bandwidth memory, while an integrated GPU usually shares memory with the system.

Most [=physical resource|physical resources=] are allocated in memory types that are efficient for GPU computation or rendering. When the user needs to provide new data to the GPU, the data may first need to cross a process boundary to reach the part of the user agent that communicates with the GPU driver. This data may then need to be made visible to the driver, so it sometimes needs to be copied into staging memory allocated by the driver. Finally, this data may need to be transferred into dedicated GPU memory, possibly changing the internal layout to the one that is most efficient for the GPU to operate on.

All these transformations are done by the user agent's WebGPU implementation.

Note: This example describes the worst case, while in practice the implementation may not need to cross a process boundary, or may be able to expose the driver-managed memory directly to the user via an `ArrayBuffer`, thus avoiding any data copies.

### Multi-Threading ### {#programming-model-multi-threading}

### Resource Usages ### {#programming-model-resource-usages}

A [=physical resource=] can be used on the GPU with an <dfn dfn>internal usage</dfn>:
<dl dfn-type=dfn dfn-for="internal usage">
    : <dfn>input</dfn>
    ::  Buffer with input data for draw or dispatch calls. Preserves contents. Allowed by buffer {{GPUBufferUsage/INDEX}}, buffer {{GPUBufferUsage/VERTEX}}, or buffer {{GPUBufferUsage/INDIRECT}}.
    : <dfn>constant</dfn>
    ::  Resource bindings that are constant from the shader's point of view. Preserves contents. Allowed by buffer {{GPUBufferUsage/UNIFORM}} or texture {{GPUTextureUsage/TEXTURE_BINDING}}.
    : <dfn>storage</dfn>
    ::  Writable storage resource binding. Allowed by buffer {{GPUBufferUsage/STORAGE}} or texture {{GPUTextureUsage/STORAGE_BINDING}}.
    : <dfn>read-only storage</dfn>
    ::  Read-only storage resource binding. Preserves contents. Allowed by buffer {{GPUBufferUsage/STORAGE}}.
    : <dfn>attachment</dfn>
    :: Texture used as an output attachment in a render pass. Allowed by texture {{GPUTextureUsage/RENDER_ATTACHMENT}}.
    : <dfn>read-only attachment</dfn>
    :: Texture used as a read-only attachment in a render pass. Allowed by texture {{GPUTextureUsage/RENDER_ATTACHMENT}}.
</dl>

A texture may consist of separate [=mipmap levels=] and [=array layers=], which can be used in different ways at any given time. Each such <dfn dfn>texture subresource</dfn> is uniquely identified by a texture, [=mipmap levels=], and (for {{GPUTextureDimension/2d}} textures only) [=array layer=] and [=aspect=].

We define a <dfn dfn>subresource</dfn> to be either a whole buffer, or a [=texture subresource=].

<div>
Some [=internal usage|internal usages=] are compatible with others. A [=subresource=] can be in a state that combines multiple usages together. We consider a list |U| to be a <dfn dfn>compatible usage list</dfn> when, and only when, any of the following rules is met:
    - Each usage in |U| is [=internal usage/input=], [=internal usage/constant=], [=internal usage/read-only storage=], or [=internal usage/read-only attachment=].
    - Each usage in |U| is [=internal usage/storage=].
    - |U| contains only a single element: [=internal usage/attachment=].
</div>

Forcing the combination of a resource's usages into only [=compatible usage list|compatible usage lists=] allows the API to limit the time during which a data race can occur when using memory. This property makes it more likely that applications written for WebGPU run on different platforms without modification.

Generally, when an implementation processes an operation that uses a [=subresource=] in a way different from the current allowed usage, the resource is transitioned to a new state. In some cases, such as within an open {{GPURenderPassEncoder}}, this transition is impossible due to hardware constraints. We define these places as <dfn dfn>usage scopes</dfn>.

The **main usage rule** is that, for any one [=subresource=], its list of [=internal usage|internal usages=] within one [=usage scope=] must be a [=compatible usage list=].

For example, binding the same buffer for [=internal usage/storage=] and [=internal usage/input=] in the same {{GPURenderPassEncoder}} would put the encoder, and the owning {{GPUCommandEncoder}}, into the error state. This combination of usages does not form a [=compatible usage list=].

Note: The race condition of having multiple writable storage buffer/texture usages within a single [=usage scope=] is allowed.

The texture [=subresource|subresources=] contained in the views provided to {{GPURenderPassColorAttachment/view|GPURenderPassColorAttachment.view}} and {{GPURenderPassColorAttachment/resolveTarget|GPURenderPassColorAttachment.resolveTarget}} are considered to be used as [=internal usage/attachment=] for the [=usage scope=] of this render pass.

The <dfn dfn>physical size</dfn> of a [=texture subresource=] is the dimension of the [=texture subresource=] in texels, including possible extra padding to form complete [=texel block|texel blocks=] in the [=subresource=].

  - For pixel-based {{GPUTextureFormat|GPUTextureFormats}}, the [=physical size=] is always equal to the size of the [=texture subresource=] used in the sampling hardware.
  - A [=texture=] in a block-based compressed {{GPUTextureFormat|GPUTextureFormats}} always has [=mipmap level=] 0 whose {{GPUTexture/[[descriptor]]}}.{{GPUTextureDescriptor/size}} is a multiple of the [=texel block size=], but a lower [=mipmap level=] may not be a multiple of the [=texel block size=], and can be padded up to an integer multiple.

<div class="example">
Consider a BC-format {{GPUTexture}} whose {{GPUTexture/[[descriptor]]}}.{{GPUTextureDescriptor/size}} is {60, 60, 1}. When sampling the {{GPUTexture}} at [=mipmap level=] 2, the sampling hardware uses {15, 15, 1} as the size of the [=texture subresource=], while its [=physical size=] is {16, 16, 1}, because the block compression algorithm can only operate on 4x4 texel blocks.
</div>

### Synchronization ### {#programming-model-synchronization}

For each [=subresource=] of a [=physical resource=], the identity set of its [=internal usage|internal usages=] is tracked on the [=queue timeline=].

Issue: This section will be revised to support multiple queues.

On the [=queue timeline=], there is an ordered sequence of [=usage scope|usage scopes=]. For the duration of each scope, the identity set of the [=internal usage|internal usages=] of any given [=subresource=] is constant. A [=subresource=] can be transitioned to a new usage at the boundary between [=usage scope|usage scopes=].

This specification defines the following [=usage scope|usage scopes=]:

- Outside of a pass (within a {{GPUCommandEncoder}}), each (non-state-setting) command is one usage scope (for example {{GPUCommandEncoder/copyBufferToTexture()}}).
- In a compute pass, each dispatch command ({{GPUComputePassEncoder/dispatch()}} or {{GPUComputePassEncoder/dispatchIndirect()}}) is one usage scope. A subresource is "used" in the usage scope if it can be accessed by the command. In a dispatch, for each bind group that can be used by the {{GPUPipelineBase/[[layout]]}} of the current {{GPUComputePipeline}}, each [=subresource=] referenced by that bind group is also "used" in the usage scope. State-setting compute pass commands, such as {{GPUProgrammablePassEncoder/setBindGroup(index, bindGroup, dynamicOffsets)}}, do not directly affect the usage scope; instead, these commands can change the state that is examined in a dispatch command.
- A render pass is one usage scope. A subresource is "used" in the usage scope if it is referenced by any (state-setting or non-state-setting) command. For example, in {{GPUProgrammablePassEncoder/setBindGroup(index, bindGroup, dynamicOffsets)}}, each subresource in `bindGroup` is "used" in the usage scope of the render pass.

Issue: The above should say [=GPU command|GPU commands=]. But we don't yet have a way to reference a specific GPU command (such as dispatch).

<div class=note>
    The above rules imply that the following example resource usages are **included** in [=usage scope validation=]:

    - Subresources used in any {{GPUProgrammablePassEncoder/setBindGroup(index, bindGroup, dynamicOffsets)|setBindGroup()}} call in a render pass, regardless of whether the bound pipeline shader or the layout actually depends on these bindings, or whether the bind group is shadowed by another "set" call.
    - Buffers used in any {{GPURenderEncoderBase/setVertexBuffer()|setVertexBuffer()}} call, regardless of whether any draw call depends on that buffer, or whether the buffer is shadowed by another "set" call.
    - Buffers used in any {{GPURenderEncoderBase/setIndexBuffer()|setIndexBuffer()}} call, regardless of whether any draw call depends on that buffer, or whether the buffer is shadowed by another "set" call.
    - Texture subresources used as color attachments, resolve attachments, or depth/stencil attachments by {{GPUCommandEncoder/beginRenderPass()|beginRenderPass()}} in {GPURenderPassDescriptor}}, regardless of whether the shader actually depends on these attachments.
    - Resources used for bind group entries whose visibility is 0, or resources visible only to the compute stage but used in a render pass (or vice versa).
</div>

During command encoding, each usage of a subresource is recorded in one of the [=usage scope|usage scopes=] of the command buffer. For each [=usage scope=], the implementation performs <dfn dfn>usage scope validation</dfn> by combining the lists of all [=internal usage=] identities from each [=subresource=] in the usage scope. If any of these lists is not a [=compatible usage list=], then {{GPUCommandEncoder/finish()|GPUCommandEncoder.finish()}} generates a {{GPUValidationError}} in the current error scope.

## Core Internal Objects ## {#core-internal-objects}

### Adapters ### {#adapters}

An <dfn dfn>adapter</dfn> identifies an implementation of WebGPU on the system: an instance of compute/rendering functionality on the platform underlying the browser, and an instance of the browser's implementation of WebGPU on top of that functionality.

An [=adapter=] does not uniquely represent the underlying implementation: calling {{GPU/requestAdapter()}} multiple times returns a different adapter object each time.

An [=adapter=] object may become [=invalid=] at any time. This happens in "[=lose the device=]" and "[=mark the adapter as stale=]". An invalid adapter cannot create new [=device|devices=].

Note: This mechanism ensures that the creation of various adapters looks similar to applications across scenarios, so it is more robust to keep more scenarios working with less testing: first initialization, re-initialization because an adapter is not plugged in, re-initialization due to a testing {{GPUDevice/destroy()|GPUDevice.destroy()}} call, and so on. This mechanism also ensures that applications use the latest system state to decide which adapter to use.

An [=adapter=] may be considered a <dfn>fallback adapter</dfn> if it has significant performance caveats in exchange for some combination of wider compatibility, more predictable behavior, or better privacy. A [=fallback adapter=] is not required to be available on every system.

An [=adapter=] has the following internal slots:
<dl dfn-type=attribute dfn-for=adapter>
    : <dfn>\[[features]]</dfn>, of type [=ordered set=]&lt;{{GPUFeatureName}}&gt;, readonly
    ::
        The [=feature|features=] that can be used to create devices on the adapter.

    : <dfn>\[[limits]]</dfn>, of type [=supported limits=], readonly
    ::
        The [=limit/better|best=] limits for creating devices on the adapter.
        Each adapter limit must be the same as or [=limit/better=] than its default value in [=supported limits=].

    : <dfn>\[[fallback]]</dfn>, of type boolean
    ::
        If set to `true`, the adapter is a [=fallback adapter=].
</dl>

An [=adapter=] is exposed via {{GPUAdapter}}.

### Devices ### {#devices}

A <dfn dfn>device</dfn> is a logical instance of an [=adapter=], through which [=internal object|internal objects=] are created. A device can be shared across multiple [=agent|agents=] (for example dedicated workers).

A [=device=] is the exclusive owner of all [=internal object|internal objects=] created from it: when the [=device=] is [=lose the device|lost=] or {{GPUDevice/destroy()|destroyed}}, the device and all objects created on it become implicitly [$valid to use with|unusable$] (directly, for example {{GPUDevice/createTexture()}}, or indirectly, for example {GPUTexture/createView()}}).

Issue: Define "ownership".

A [=device=] has the following internal slots:

<dl dfn-type=attribute dfn-for=device>
    : <dfn>\[[adapter]]</dfn>, of type [=adapter=], readonly
    ::
        The [=adapter=] from which this device was created.

    : <dfn>\[[features]]</dfn>, of type [=ordered set=]&lt;{{GPUFeatureName}}&gt;, readonly
    ::
        The [=feature|features=] that can be used on this device. No additional features can be used, even if the underlying [=adapter=] can support them.

    : <dfn>\[[limits]]</dfn>, of type [=supported limits=], readonly
    ::
        The limits that can be used on this device. No [=limit/better=] limits can be used, even if the underlying [=adapter=] can support them.
</dl>

<div>
    When creating a <dfn dfn>new device</dfn> from an [=adapter=] using a {{GPUDeviceDescriptor}} descriptor:

    - Set |device|.{{device/[[adapter]]}} to |adapter|.

    - Set |device|.{{device/[[features]]}} to the [=ordered set|set=] of values in |descriptor|.{{GPUDeviceDescriptor/requiredFeatures}}.

    - Let |device|.{{device/[[limits]]}} be a [=supported limits=] object with the default values. For each (|key|, |value|) pair in |descriptor|.{{GPUDeviceDescriptor/requiredLimits}}, set the member of |device|.{{device/[[limits]]}} corresponding to key to the [=limit/better=] of |value| or the default value in [=supported limits=].
</div>

Any time the user agent needs to revoke access to a device, it calls [=lose the device=](device, `undefined`).

<div>
    <dfn dfn>lose the device</dfn>(|device|, |reason|):

    1. Make |device|.{{device/[[adapter]]}} [=invalid=].
    2. Make |device| [=invalid=].
    3. Issue: Explain how to get its "primary" {{GPUDevice}} from |device|.
    4. Resolve |device|.{{GPUDevice/lost}} with a new {{GPUDeviceLostInfo}}, with
        {{GPUDeviceLostInfo/reason}} set to |reason|, and
        {{GPUDeviceLostInfo/message}} set to an implementation-defined value.

        Note: {{GPUDeviceLostInfo/message}} should not disclose unnecessary user/system information, and should never be parsed by applications.
</div>

A [=device=] is exposed via {{GPUDevice}}.

## Optional Capabilities ## {#optional-capabilities}

WebGPU [=adapter|adapters=] and [=device|devices=] have <dfn dfn>capabilities</dfn> that describe WebGPU functionality that differs between implementations, usually due to hardware or system software constraints. A [=capabilities|capability=] is either a [=feature=] or a [=limit=].

### Features ### {#features}

A <dfn dfn>feature</dfn> is a set of optional WebGPU functionality that is not supported by all implementations, usually due to hardware or system software constraints.

Each {{GPUAdapter}} exposes a set of available features. Only these features may be requested in {{GPUAdapter/requestDevice()}}.

The functionality that is part of a feature can only be used if that feature is requested at device creation time. Dictionary members added to existing dictionaries by an optional feature are always optional at the WebIDL level; if the feature is not enabled, such members must not be set to a non-default value.

Note: Although enabling a feature does not add new IDL-required fields, it may not necessarily be backward compatible with existing code. An optional feature can enable new validation that makes previously-valid code invalid.

For a description of the functionality enabled by each feature, see the [[#feature-index|Feature Index]].

### Limits ### {#limits}

Each <dfn dfn>limit</dfn> is a numeric limit on the use of WebGPU on a device.

A <dfn dfn>supported limits</dfn> object has a value for every defined limit. Each [=adapter=] has a set of [=supported limits=], and each {{GPUDeviceDescriptor/requiredLimits|created}} [=device=] has its own specific [=supported limits=]. Device limits are enforced regardless of the adapter's limits.

Each limit has a <dfn dfn for=limit>default</dfn> value. Each [=adapter=] is guaranteed to support the default value or [=limit/better=]. If a value is not explicitly specified in {{GPUDeviceDescriptor/requiredLimits}}, the default value is used.

One limit value may be <dfn dfn for=limit>better</dfn> than another. A [=limit/better=] limit value always relaxes validation, thus making more programs valid. "Better" is defined for each [=limit class=].

Different limits have different <dfn dfn lt="limit class">limit classes</dfn>:

<dl dfn-type=dfn dfn-for="limit class">
    : <dfn>maximum</dfn>
    ::
        The limit enforces a maximum on some value passed to the API.

        Higher is [=limit/better=].

        May only be set to a value ≥ the [=limit/default=] value. Lower values are clamped to the [=limit/default=] value.

    : <dfn>alignment</dfn>
    ::
        The limit enforces a minimum alignment on some value passed to the API; that is, the value must be a multiple of the limit.

        Smaller is [=limit/better=].

        May only be set to a value ≤ the [=limit/default=] value that is a power of 2. Values that are not powers of 2 are invalid. Higher powers of 2 are clamped to the [=limit/default=] value.
</dl>

Note: Setting "better" limits is not necessarily desirable, as they may impact performance. Therefore, for better portability across devices and implementations, applications should generally request the "worst" limits that work for their content (ideally, the default values).

<table class="data no-colspan-center" dfn-type=attribute dfn-for="supported limits">
    <thead>
        <tr><th>Limit name <th>Type <th>[=limit class=] <th>[=limit/default=] value
    </thead>

    <tr><td><dfn>maxTextureDimension1D</dfn>
        <td>{{GPUSize32}} <td>[=limit class/maximum=]<td>8192
    <tr class=row-continuation><td colspan=4>
        The maximum allowed {{GPUTextureDescriptor/size}}.[=Extent3D/width=] for a [=texture=] created with {{GPUTextureDescriptor/dimension}} {{GPUTextureDimension/"1d"}}.

    <tr><td><dfn>maxTextureDimension2D</dfn>
        <td>{{GPUSize32}} <td>[=limit class/maximum=]<td>8192
    <tr class=row-continuation><td colspan=4>
        The maximum allowed {{GPUTextureDescriptor/size}}.[=Extent3D/width=] and {{GPUTextureDescriptor/size}}.[=Extent3D/height=] for a [=texture=] created with {{GPUTextureDescriptor/dimension}} {{GPUTextureDimension/"2d"}}.

    <tr><td><dfn>maxTextureDimension3D</dfn>
        <td>{{GPUSize32}} <td>[=limit class/maximum=] <td>2048
    <tr class=row-continuation><td colspan=4>
        The maximum allowed {{GPUTextureDescriptor/size}}.[=Extent3D/width=], {{GPUTextureDescriptor/size}}.[=Extent3D/height=] and {{GPUTextureDescriptor/size}}.[=Extent3D/depthOrArrayLayers=] for a [=texture=] created with {{GPUTextureDescriptor/dimension}} {{GPUTextureDimension/"3d"}}.

    <tr><td><dfn>maxTextureArrayLayers</dfn>
        <td>{{GPUSize32}} <td>[=limit class/maximum=] <td>256
    <tr class=row-continuation><td colspan=4>
        The maximum allowed {{GPUTextureDescriptor/size}}.[=Extent3D/depthOrArrayLayers=] for a [=texture=] created with {{GPUTextureDescriptor/dimension}} {{GPUTextureDimension/"1d"}} or {{GPUTextureDimension/"2d"}}.

    <tr><td><dfn>maxBindGroups</dfn>
        <td>{{GPUSize32}} <td>[=limit class/maximum=] <td>4
